<ArticleLayout slug="china-ecommerce-content-pack" ctaLabel="Get a like-for-like scope" faq={faq}>
  <p class="standfirst">Ask three studios to quote "China ecommerce content" and you get three prices for three different jobs. Nobody notices until delivery, when one pack has a mobile detail page and another does not. The fix is a shared list, and nobody has published one.</p>

  <h2>What is in a China ecommerce content pack?</h2>

  <p>A listing on Tmall or JD is built from the same named parts: a main image set with a white-background image inside it, a long desktop detail page, a separate mobile detail page, a size and specification chart, store decoration banners, and short video. The parts are stable across the sources that describe them. The dimensions attached to them are not, which is exactly where quotes quietly diverge.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Every Chinese production reference checked builds a listing from the same named parts: a main image set with a pure-white-background image inside it, a long scrolling detail page, a separate mobile detail page, a size and specification chart, store decoration banners, and short video. The parts are stable. The dimensions attached to them are not.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: eight independent Chinese design, tool and seller-education references collected September 2026, compared on their deliverable lists only, with every number they carried set aside.</p>

  <p>That split, stable parts and unstable numbers, is the whole method of this page. The list below is fixed. The numbers live in separate, counted, dated articles.</p>

  <h2>Why two quotes for the same SKU describe different jobs</h2>

  <p>Partly because the rules a quote should be built to cannot be read from outside a seller account.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The two platform rule centers a buyer would check are published at public web addresses and serve no rule text without a seller session. On 10 September 2026 the Tmall rule center redirected twice and returned no rule body, and two different JD merchant rule pages returned the rule-center heading and nothing else.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: direct unauthenticated fetches of rule.tmall.com and rule.jd.com, 10 September 2026, four requests across two platforms, redirect chain and response body recorded.</p>

  <p>So each supplier quotes to its own reading of the specs, and to its own idea of what a listing includes. Nobody is being dishonest. They are just not quoting the same thing.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A search across five buyer phrasings in English and Chinese returned no page that itemizes what one China ecommerce SKU needs across Tmall and JD. English results answered for Western marketplaces or for parcel logistics. Chinese results carried numbers with no link to the rule that sets them.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: five queries run September 2026, English and Chinese, every returned result classified by page type and by what it answered.</p>

  <h2>The manifest for one SKU across Tmall and JD</h2>

  <p>This is a scoping template, not a platform requirement. It names the parts a listing on both platforms is built from, so every quote can be read against the same rows. The counts and sizes for each part are deliberately not printed here, because they are contested and they move. Each row points to where the counted, dated figures live.</p>

