  <p>One row is worth pulling out, because it is the only place the two platforms genuinely differ on the evidence published. The article comparing JD image requirements with Tmall counts five of five sources per platform putting the white-background image first in JD's carousel and fifth in Tmall's. The image itself is shared. Only its position changes, which is a re-sequence rather than a second shoot.</p>

  <h2>Naming conventions and file structure</h2>

  <p>No platform publishes a naming standard for these files, so what follows is production practice, not a rule. Its only job is to let a buyer, a supplier and a store operator find the same file without asking.</p>

  <p>A workable convention puts the stable identifiers first and the variable ones last: the SKU code, then the platform, then the deliverable, then the slot or section, then the version. A white-background image for one SKU on JD reads as SKU code, JD, white background, slot one, version two. The same image on Tmall changes one field, the slot, and nothing else.</p>

  <p>Folders follow the same order. One folder per SKU, one subfolder per platform where the files genuinely differ, and one shared folder for everything that is identical on both, so a change to a shared master is made once rather than twice.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
