  <h2>What a complete handover contains</h2>

  <p>This is where quotes differ most and where buyers look least. A price for "the listing images" can mean flattened exports only, or everything needed to change them later. Ask which, in writing, before comparing prices.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Handover item</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it matters</th>
          <th scope="col">Ask the supplier</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Final exports per platform</th>
          <td>What actually uploads</td>
          <td>Are both Tmall and JD sets included, or one set</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Layered source files</th>
          <td>Editable masters for later changes</td>
          <td>Are layered files delivered, or kept by the supplier</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Raw captures</th>
          <td>The material a future variant is built from</td>
          <td>Is raw capture included, and in what form</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Metadata and naming</th>
          <td>Findability across the SKU range</td>
          <td>Does the naming follow a stated convention</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Provenance record</th>
          <td>What was captured, generated and edited, per asset</td>
          <td>Is a per-asset record delivered with the files</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Retention terms</th>
          <td>How long source files exist after delivery</td>
          <td>When are working files deleted, and can that be extended</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>The provenance row matters more each year. The article on the disclosure audit trail covers what a per-asset record should hold, field by field, and which platform labeling rules it answers.</p>

  <h2>How long are production files retained?</h2>

  <p>As long as each supplier's policy says, because no rule sets it.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Retention of production files is a policy each supplier sets, not a legal requirement. hubStudio purges source files from its working environments 90 days after a project closes unless the client asks for them to be kept.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio's ecommerce design service page, read 10 September 2026, stated as published studio policy rather than as a benchmark.</p>

  <p>That is one studio's policy, and the point of printing it is the question it raises for every other quote. If layered source files are deleted after a fixed window, a buyer who has not taken delivery of them has lost the ability to change the listing without paying for it again. Ask for the retention window in writing, and take the source files before it closes if the listing will need updates.</p>

  <h2>How do I compare two China content quotes?</h2>

  <p>Put both quotes against the same rows. Where one quote is silent on a row, treat it as excluded until the supplier says otherwise.</p>

