  <h2>Questions buyers ask about China content packs</h2>

  <h3>What is in a China ecommerce content pack?</h3>

  <p>A main image set with a white-background image inside it, a desktop detail page, a separate mobile detail page, a size and specification chart, store decoration banners, and short video. Eight Chinese production references describe listings from those same parts. The dimensions attached to each part vary by source, so they belong in a dated spec reference rather than a fixed list.</p>

  <h3>How many assets does one SKU need for Tmall and JD?</h3>

  <p>No published source gives a verified per-SKU count, and treating any count as a platform requirement would overstate it. What holds is the list of deliverables: the same named parts on both platforms, with the white-background image sitting in a different carousel slot on each. Scope the count with your supplier against the manifest.</p>

  <h3>Should a handover include layered source files?</h3>

  <p>If the listing will ever change, yes. Flattened exports upload fine but cannot be edited cleanly, so a later variant means paying to rebuild. Ask in writing whether layered files and raw captures are delivered or retained by the supplier, and take them before any retention window closes.</p>

  <h3>How long are production files retained?</h3>

  <p>It is a supplier policy, not a legal requirement, so it varies. hubStudio's published policy is to purge source files 90 days after a project closes unless the client asks for them to be kept. Whatever the supplier, get the window in writing and take delivery of the files you may need before it ends.</p>

  <h3>How do I compare two China content quotes?</h3>

  <p>Hold both against the same manifest rows: main images for both platforms, the white-background image, separate desktop and mobile detail pages, the spec chart, banners, video, layered source files, a provenance record and a written retention window. Treat anything a quote does not mention as excluded until confirmed.</p>
